# Former Gods, Part 11
Tamil title: மாஜி கடவுள்கள் - பகுதி 11

...were born, their domination vanished and true knowledge dawned. We consider it necessary to explain how the doctrine of a single God, who has neither a vehicle to ride nor wives to embrace, came to be established. This explanation will also shatter the false claim that the poets who lived here in former times possessed an imaginative power unmatched anywhere else in the world.

23-17-47

Among the gods worshipped by the Egyptians, Ra was regarded as the primal and supreme god. He was approximately like the Sun portrayed in our mythology. Ra emerged radiantly from an egg, and that egg arose from the sea. But whence came that sea? No one asked. Until the window of reason opened, Egyptian priests called such questioning atheism, and the people believed that this sea-born egg caused the creation of the universe.

## The Frog-Faced Goddess

## The Egyptian Creation of the Universe

The sea, wind, sky, stars, sun and moon, thunder, lightning, and torrential rain were strange and inexplicable to humankind before knowledge developed. These sights stood before the eyes, but their meaning was unknown and their causes unexplained. The breeze brought delight, while the storm brought ruin. The sun supplied light but at times also scorching heat. The moon repeatedly waned and waxed. The stars seemed to twinkle mockingly: “O human, tell us where we are, who we are, and whether you have seen how high above you we stand. Can you reach us here?”

Every natural object and event brought either benefit or harm, yet humanity possessed no explanation for it. People could not disregard them because each affected human life: the effect was visible, but the meaning was not. They gazed upward in wonder, looked upon the surging sea without knowing its farther shore, and trembled before terrible thunder and blinding lightning.

This condition prevailed everywhere before knowledge grew. People sought an explanation for everything. What was the source of all things? What came first: sea, wind, sky, earth, human, animal, or plant? Who arranged them? What arose from what, and why? The mind became confused by these questions. Wind was mighty enough to uproot great trees and toss them like balls. The sea seemed stranger still, the sky stranger than all, and the sun supremely powerful. Every natural thing inspired wonder, but no explanation could be found.

When humanity generally remained in that state, a few people pondered these mysteries without cease and offered explanations according to their ability. Those explanations became fantastic tales. Fraudsters and priestly groups then used them to deceive ordinary people, make a living, and establish their authority.

Numerous imagined stories were devised for incomprehensible problems. Most concerned the creation of the universe and the fate of a person after death. They dealt chiefly with the two ends called the distant past and the future. Every country told its own story of creation, and in Egypt too one such fantasy ruled people’s minds for ages.

There was first a vast, turbulent sea whose depth, extent, and course no one knew. Before sky, earth, grass, plant, heaven, hell, god, or demon appeared, only that great sea existed in commotion. From it arose a shining egg, and from that egg emerged the first god, Ra.

The sea from which Ra was born was called Nu. Ra had three sacred names. At dawn, when he spread golden rays, he was Khepera; by day he was Ra; and in the evening he bore another name. Egyptians celebrated him as God of gods and the primal deity for many centuries. As here, there too temples, priests, festivals, worship, and splendour flourished.

Egyptian mythology said that Ra created all gods: Shu, god of air; the lion-faced goddess Tefnut; Seb, god of earth; Nut, goddess of the sky; and many others. Nut bore four children, two male and two female: Osiris and Isis, Set and Nephthys. At Ra’s command, sky and earth emerged from the primordial sea. Ra created human beings from his eyes, just as our mythology says the Brahmin arose from Brahma’s face.

It existed in Egypt; it still exists here. When knowledge began to rule Egypt, people gained the courage and clarity to call tales of a primal god appearing as an egg and producing many gods inventions. Like many other countries, they attained the higher truth of one formless God. Here, however, stories still dominate of Sri Narayana reclining in mystic sleep, a lotus stalk growing from his navel, four-faced Brahma sitting upon its flower, and Saraswati dwelling upon Brahma’s tongue. Whoever denies them becomes a great sinner.

On one side stands the simpleton who believes the story literally. On another stands the schemer who assigns it a philosophical meaning. Elsewhere are the acrobats of “science” who claim to discover truths buried in it. On yet another side is the priesthood threatening that belief in these tales alone is theism and asking for reasons is atheism. Egyptians once lived in this same condition, but when knowledge came they consigned to the rubbish heap the ancient tales that the sun was an egg born from the sea and that its children were the many gods.

Ra was the god of an age without inquiry. Mythic poets praised his power, artists depicted his beauty in verse and painting, and ordinary people piled offerings before him like a mountain. He ruled with enormous influence until knowledge emerged; then Ra became a former god.

Before that happened, the Egyptian counterparts of Kalidasa and Kamban adorned him with many garlands of epic verse, and common people fell before him in worship. According to Egyptian mythology, Ra performed many acts of grace capable of melting their hearts. Creator of the earth, he also became its king and ruled lovingly with his people’s welfare as his sole aim.

While Ra lived on earth, Isis was there too. She coveted the omnipotence that Ra possessed and continually plotted to obtain authority. Even beginners in the university of reason would feel like clapping and laughing. Rivalry, jealousy, greed, intrigue, and lust for power are qualities of the lowest human types. How could Isis, who possessed them, be a goddess? How did Egyptians make room in their minds to worship her? Today one would ask these questions; then, under mental delusion, people celebrated them as sacred virtues.

Ra’s omnipotence supposedly came from a magical name inscribed in his heart, known to no one else. Isis believed that learning it would make her as powerful as Ra and waited for her opportunity.

Ra grew old and staggered as he walked. A phlegmy cough overtook him; saliva ran from his mouth; grey hair, wrinkles, and extreme age overcame the primal god. Today one asks whether growing decrepit is divine, but courage to ask had not yet been born, for knowledge was still in the womb.

Seeing Ra’s spittle fall upon the ground, Isis took the earth and fashioned an invisible magic serpent. Even Ra, God of gods, could not see it. It bit him, and he writhed in unbearable pain without knowing its cause. Near death, he summoned all the gods, his children, and lamented: “Dear children, my life is convulsing. I cannot bear this agony, and I do not know its cause. Fire did not touch me, yet I feel as though I have fallen into it; my body has turned cold. What shall I do? How can I survive?”

All the gods wailed except Isis, who showed no pity. Gods versed in spells chanted to remove Ra’s pain, to no avail. Others gave medicine, but the great lord did not recover. Isis then declared that she could cure him, provided he promised to grant her desire.

The primal god’s life was ebbing while a greedy goddess bargained. “What do you need? Tell me, child,” pleaded Ra. “I must know the magic name inscribed in your heart,” she replied. Ra protested that no one could or should know it, for only its presence in his heart made him Lord of all. Yet the Lord of all lay dying. This assertion, utterly incompatible with reason, was Egyptian theism of that age.

Having no alternative, Ra accepted Isis’s condition. The magic name passed into her mind, and the deadly poison of the magic serpent left the great lord. The primal god survived.

Today we readily ask how a god could be poisoned, imperilled, and then rescued, and who could believe such an invention. But people once revered these as divine mysteries. Every country possessed such stories: a god falling ill, facing danger, growing old, or lying near death. Refusal to believe made one an atheist. Egyptians today no longer believe Ra’s suffering from a magic serpent. Once knowledge shone, they cast aside the meaningless tale.

But here? Listen closely even now to the mythologist singing: “When my father, the Kailasa-dweller and beloved of Uma, swallowed the Halahala poison, the surrounding gods trembled. They feared it would enter Siva and consume his life. Parvati, seeing the peril that befell her husband, embraced him and cried, ‘Lord, what shall I do?’ Through the power of the Mother’s arm around his neck, the poison could not pass inward and remained in his throat. Siva survived and became Nilakantha.”

In Egypt today one cannot find people reading that Ra nearly died from a magic serpent’s bite and was saved by Isis. True knowledge arose and rejected the falsehood; Ra became a former god. Here the tale of swallowing Halahala still rules, and many condemn its denier as a great sinner.

Even after rescue from death, the great lord’s troubles did not cease. Some humans mocked him: “How long must we remain under this old fellow’s rule? He will not even die; although decrepit, he remains king. There is no use trusting this old god any longer.”

Unable to bear the grief, Ra summoned the gods and said: “Some wicked people oppose and ridicule me and revile my rule. What shall be done? With your consent I shall destroy these insolent evildoers.” All agreed. Ra’s eye immediately assumed the form of the goddess Hathor and slaughtered the offenders in a hunt for blood.

The criminals died, but Hathor became accustomed to drinking blood and continued killing humans in frenzy. Ra relented and planned to recall her. He mixed barley, an Egyptian foodstuff, with blood and filled seven thousand jars. After drinking it, Hathor’s rage subsided and she once more became Ra’s eye. The drink Ra prepared to quell the destructive goddess’s bloodlust later became the beverage called beer, so the story says.

After destroying the wicked, Ra decided it was no longer proper to remain king of earth. He created heaven, called Aalu, and went there to live. On earth, Osiris and his wife Isis then ruled.

Osiris’s reign was a golden age. He reformed people who had lived as savages and taught them agriculture, industry, education, and art. His subjects benefited, but his younger brother Set, an evil god, resented him. Set befriended seventy-two ruffians from Ethiopia, and the group plotted to eliminate Osiris and make Set king.

They arranged a great banquet for Osiris, supposedly to celebrate victory. Set displayed a magnificent chest that many guests desired and announced that it would belong to whoever fitted it exactly. Many lay inside but did not fit. Osiris did. At once Set shut the lid, nailed it down, threw the chest into the Nile, destroyed his opponents, and proclaimed himself king.

Hearing Osiris’s fate, Isis wailed and cursed the tyrant. When Set tried to destroy her too, she resolved to find the chest containing her husband’s body and searched all along the Nile. Two divinely blessed scorpions accompanied the goddess: scorpions were a goddess’s helpers.

The chest drifted to the Syrian coast and became enclosed within a magic tree. A local king marvelled at the tree, had it cut down, and installed it as a pillar in his palace, with the chest and Osiris’s body still inside.

While Isis wandered in search of her husband, a son named Horus was born to this Egyptian Savitri. The Egyptian Kamsa plotted to kill the child, so she entrusted him to a god in serpent form and continued her search. At last a disembodied voice revealed that the chest was in the region of Byblos in Syria. After many ordeals she recovered it.

Set discovered this, cut Osiris’s body into fourteen pieces, and threw them into the Nile. Crocodiles refused to eat them, though a fish swallowed one small part. Isis found all the remaining pieces, joined them, and buried them in a chest. This supposedly gave Egypt its custom of preserving dead bodies and interring them in coffins. When Horus grew to manhood, he drove Set away and established his own rule; Set became lord of hell.

Such a “story” was invented, praised as sacred, and used to sustain worship of its gods in Egypt for ages. These tales provoke laughter on hearing them; they have neither head nor foot, reason nor coherence. Yet they were once Egypt’s divine legends. Poets sang the gods’ sacred sports and people built enormous temples, all before intellectual clarity arose. Reason then developed, and Ra, Osiris, and Isis became former gods there, though not here.

What is honoured here is not the history of humanity’s arduous intellectual progress from blind belief to reason. Fantasies devised by some deceiver in some remote age for the gullible are honoured instead. Nor can we say that only dull-witted people without minds of their own wallow in this mythological mire. Even those decorated with the title of intellectual supply some philosophical meaning and remain entangled in antiquity. Some additionally boast: “Let mythology be false; let us even agree that it is no longer needed. But can we fail to rejoice at the wonderful imaginative power of our ancestors revealed in it?”

Egypt, Babylon, and many other lands possessed imagination, epic and pictorial art, and philosophical interpretation in ample measure. Their creations were nevertheless judged products of unenlightened thought and rejected, a fact our “intellectuals” do not tell most of our people. They boast as though tales of cosmic creation, divine incarnations, heaven, and hell flowered only here and no other country possessed imagination. Seeds sown by fraudsters in the field of mythology produced strange crops everywhere.

Do the Babylonian, Teutonic, and Egyptian creation tales lack flavour, description, simile, moving incidents, or astonishing divine sports? The Egyptian mythologist lied even more extravagantly about Isis’s efforts to recover Osiris than our puranic tale does about Savitri’s efforts to recover Satyavan. Did Egyptians argue, like antiquity-mad people here, that myths must be preserved at least for their imaginative ornament? They threw out the old rubbish. Was their country ruined, or did their people lose morality? No; knowledge rules there.

Only here do people continue to place the glory of the tortoise and boar incarnations upon their heads. Even in imagining heaven and hell, Egyptian mythology successfully rivals ours.

In Egyptian mythology heaven is the abode of the virtuous. There is a place in the upper world to weigh sin and virtue, with a separate god assigned to it. After death, a person’s soul journeys upward in a description that surpasses even our mythology in the scale of its falsehood.

The soul supposedly sets out carrying enough food. With its bundle of provisions, it must cross forest, mountain, desert, and regions roamed by terrifying animals, then pass the western mountain. There stands a gigantic tree. From its base appears a goddess bearing trays of fruit and sweets, and the soul must accept her feast.

It must drive away a tortoise as large as a mountain, kill great snakes advancing with open mouths, and overcome many creatures of fearful appearance. Set arrives with a camel’s head, dog’s body, serpent’s tail, and red colour; the soul must repel him too.

A magic boat then appears. Some gods sit inside, but neither speak nor help. The boat itself, not its boatmen, asks questions and moves only when given true answers. The soul can then reach the court.

There Osiris sits upon a throne conducting the inquiry, surrounded by forty-two gods, all in animal form. The soul must declare that it committed no offence during life. Once that testimony is accepted as true, the jackal-faced god Anubis takes it by the hand and leads it before Osiris.

The court contains a balance. The soul’s heart is placed in one pan and an ostrich feather in the other. If they balance, the soul reaches heaven. Otherwise, the forty-two...

