This installment begins in the middle of a sentence continued from `maji_kadavulgal10.md`; the leading ellipsis is supplied only in the English translation to signal that boundary. It ends in the middle of a sentence, which continues directly with “animal gods...” in `maji_kadavulgal12.md`.

The printed/OCR date appears as `23- 17-47`, an impossible month value. It is retained unchanged in the Tamil source and reproduced conservatively in the translation rather than silently emended.

Several Egyptian names follow the Tamil source’s older transliterations: `ரா` (Ra), `நூ` (Nu), `கெப்பீரா` (Khepera), `ஷு` (Shu), `டெப்னட்` (Tefnut), `செப்` (Seb), `நட்` (Nut), `ஆசரிஸ்` (Osiris), `இசிஸ்` (Isis), `செட்` (Set), `நெப்தீஸ்` (Nephthys), `ஹாதார்` (Hathor), `ஹோரஸ்` (Horus), and `அனுபிஸ்` (Anubis). The English uses their conventional spellings without changing the retained Tamil OCR.

The evening name of Ra is visibly damaged in the OCR (`மாலையிலேடும்`); no unsupported name has been invented in the translation.
